<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How do TikTok ads work?</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading --></div>
<!-- /wp:column -->

<!-- wp:column {"width":"60%"} -->
<div class="wp-block-column" style="flex-basis:60%"><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>TikTok shows short videos, one after another, chosen by what each person tends to watch. Adverts appear in that same stream. People decide in a second or two whether to keep watching, and one swipe ends it.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>That is why the opening matters more than anything else. The first line or image, called the hook, has to give a reason to stay: a problem the viewer recognises, a surprising result, a question. Polished studio adverts tend to do worse than a person talking to a phone, because they look like something to skip.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Behind the videos is an advertising system much like Meta's. You choose an objective, an audience and a daily budget, and a small piece of tracking code on your site, the TikTok pixel, reports which adverts led to sales. Most of the work is testing several hooks and keeping the ones that pay.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div>
<!-- /wp:column --></div>
<!-- /wp:columns --></section>
<!-- /wp:group -->

